          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-5950ab1bef9393e9\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Antwan’s “I love you” is principally an avowal that he has loving feelings for Nikki. Its placement within a plea for another chance gives it slight undertaking-like force, but the sentence itself neither clearly commits him to a course of action nor establishes an obligation. His emotional tone and urgency intensify the avowal without independently showing that the words are a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is limited ambiguity over whether the declaration helps enact renewed romantic commitment; the surrounding language more clearly requests reconciliation than undertakes it.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Antwan originally speaks the words into a voicemail; the passage quotes the recorded utterance as Nikki plays it back.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration occurs in one of many voicemails pleading for renewed access to Nikki after Antwan has hurt her. “I need you” and “Just give me a chance” make the avowal part of an urgent attempt at reconciliation. The narration treats his love and pain as real, while Nikki interprets the broader appeal as a pull back into a relationship entangled with his girlfriend, children, and existing responsibilities. This pressure and emotional intensity shape the declaration’s effect on her but do not alter its predominantly truth-conditional core force or establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“But please don’t shut me out. I love you. I need you. Just give me a chance.”\",\"supports\":\"The isolated declarative directly avows love, while the adjacent requests supply weak contextual support for relational or undertaking force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The message follows 22 missed voicemails, including “I love you… I want to be with you” and requests that Nikki call him back or let him in.\",\"supports\":\"The broader speech event is an urgent courtship and reconciliation appeal, though repetition and urgency alone do not establish E or strong P.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“She could hear his pain, feel the longing in his voice,” and the narration earlier says, “There was love in his words. Real love.”\",\"supports\":\"The narrative presents the declaration as an emotionally intense and apparently sincere avowal; pain and vocal emotion remain delivery context rather than independent evidence of reflex-like expressive force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Nikki recalls “his family, his girlfriend, his children” and refuses to return to a world where love is “tangled in responsibilities and promises to others.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration has substantial relational consequences for Nikki, but those complications belong to context rather than to a missing dimension of the utterance’s core force.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the central avowal, with slight P support from its use in seeking renewed relationship and no independent basis for E or O. The surrounding emotional pressure and relational conflict are adequately handled as context.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
